When truncating strings containing Unicode characters, using string.length can lead to incorrect results as it counts UTF-16 code units rather than actual characters. Instead, spread the string into an array ([...string]) to properly handle Unicode graphemes. This ensures correct truncation of multi-byte characters like Japanese or emoji characters. Always consider using grapheme-based operations when working with international text to maintain character integrity across different writing systems.

